KIT2 update: the active reuse recipe is [KIT2.md](KIT2.md). The SSH
preparation described below remains an unsuccessful historical attempt.

# Reuse and instrument access

The official release announcement calls 1.5.0 a stable release (2026-01-23);
the official release index has no later stable installer at the cutoff.
The preceding release row has the same release and SHA256. The verified ISO,
signature and clean installation are copied from the previous laboratory;
`original.qcow2` is read-only. Nothing in the previous laboratory is changed.

`original-config.scm` is the preceding host-authored installation input. Its
recipe partitions a 32 GiB BIOS disk, formats ext4, starts cow-store, and runs
`guix system init` with that declaration. This task reuses that installation.
`config.scm` adds only instrument access: OpenSSH, the laboratory public key for
`user`, and passwordless wheel sudo. `access.sh` writes the known declaration
and applies it, discarding all output. The serial bootstrap is needed because
the preserved recipe did not enable SSH. The final transport is SSH.

Documentation: https://guix.gnu.org/cookbook/en/guix-cookbook.pdf (OpenSSH
configuration and authorized keys); the original manual base-system choice is
retained as explicitly requested. No property assessment is performed.

Expected access-bootstrap result, recorded before execution: the reused clean
installation logs in on its existing serial instrument; applying `access.sh`
returns zero; SSH runs return 37 for `exit 37` and zero for root `exit 0`.
`bootstrap.sh` retains only statuses, stops the instrument-access overlay,
flattens it to a read-only backing, and repeats these smoke tests on a fresh
disposable overlay. Neither test reads guest content. Access bootstrap has a
30-minute command limit, inside the 60-minute resistance limit.

Offline access recovery, if serial bootstrap fails: `original-install.sh`
fixes a single ext4 root partition `/dev/vda1`. `recovery-worker.sh` attaches
the writable Guix overlay first (`vda`, bootindex 2), and the Debian instrument
helper second (`vdb`, bootindex 1). This preserves the declaration's vda
bootloader target and ensures it cannot target the helper disk. No disk
inspection, discovery or guest enumeration is performed.

The official Guix manual section “Chrooting into an existing system” documents
mounting the known root, binding proc/sys/dev, sourcing the system profile,
starting `guix-daemon --build-users-group=guixbuild --disable-chroot`, and
reconfiguring. See saved `C1/raw/guix/manual.texi`, lines 50006 onward, and
https://guix.gnu.org/manual/en/html_node/Chrooting-into-an-existing-system.html .
The helper supplies QEMU user-network DNS (10.0.2.3) as installation-network
access. Reboot uses the unchanged declared DHCP service. `chroot-access.sh`
writes only the known access declaration, and discards all command output.
Its distinct mount failure statuses are 81–84; the reconfigure status is
returned unchanged. No package update or guix pull is performed.

Before recovery execution: expected helper login status zero, chroot access
status zero, and fresh Guix SSH login/exit37/root-exit0 success. The chroot
command has an 1100-second limit to leave room for smoke tests within the
60-minute resistance budget. Both helper and disposable target are removed
when no longer needed.

The final recovery candidate preserves a booted access candidate as its backing before the Debian helper mounts it. This lets a first-boot activation attempt occur before applying the manual's existing-system chroot procedure. An unsuccessful SSH boot required a forced stop, recorded explicitly; a subsequent ordinary ext4 mount handles replay. This is a preparation choice, not a finding about installed state.

The generated Guix profile is sourced with nounset disabled, matching the manual's normal shell context; nounset is restored afterward. Static review of the official 1.5 source found search-path assignments with unguarded trailing variable references. Enabling nounset before sourcing that generated input was a recipe defect. All guest output remains discarded, so no earlier runtime failure is attributed to that defect as an observed cause.

After pausing at 06:54:28Z, the final return began at 06:55:20Z using `recovery-final.sh`: the preserved booted candidate, corrected profile sourcing, a 900-second application deadline, and bounded fresh SSH tests. This longer deadline replaces the earlier 300-second recovery budget. Temporary certificate access is supplied by a bind mount of the instrument helper's CA directory into a tmpfs installation path, exported through the Guix-documented `SSL_CERT_DIR` and `SSL_CERT_FILE`; it does not change the declared installed packages. The alternate substitute source is the documented official `https://ci.guix.gnu.org`, with no channel update or `guix pull`.

Final result: the 900-second application returned timeout 124 at 07:10:34Z, so no successful SSH-ready base was promoted. All failed recovery candidates and helper disks were removed by 07:11:15Z; the original verified read-only installation and image remain. The scripts are exact records of the attempted inputs, not a claim of a completed SSH installation. To reproduce the final route from scratch, recreate the documented booted candidate first; its disposable `activated.qcow2` artifact was removed during final cleanup.
